# T1110 — Brute Force: High-Volume Failed Logons from Single IP

## Summary

Detects automated brute force against exposed authentication services by flagging any single public source IP that generates 5+ failed logon events within a 1-hour window.

| Attribute | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| MITRE ATT&CK | [T1110 — Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/) |
| Severity | Medium |
| Platform | Windows |
| Source log | SecurityEvent (Event ID 4625) |
| Sigma rule | [rule.yml](rule.yml) |
| KQL rule | [rule.kql](rule.kql) |
| Status | Deployed in Sentinel |

## Detection logic

Rolling 1-hour window per source IP, counting failed logon events. IPs matching:

- EventID 4625 (failed logon), AND
- Public IP (excludes `10.0.0.0/8`, `192.168.0.0/16`, and empty/dash values), AND
- 5 or more failed attempts in a 1-hour window


## Entity mapping

The rule maps the `IpAddress` field to a Sentinel `IP` entity with the `Address` identifier. This enables:

- Automatic enrichment of alerts with Threat Intelligence Indicators
- Automatic correlation with other alerts involving the same IP
- SOAR playbook input (e.g., auto-block at firewall via Sentinel automation rule)

## MITRE ATT&CK mapping

- **Tactic:** Credential Access (TA0006)
- **Technique:** Brute Force (T1110)
- **Sub-techniques that could also trigger this rule:** T1110.001 (Password Guessing)


## False positives

On production Windows servers, users who forget their password and repeatedly mistype it could trigger this. On an exposed honeypot with no legitimate users, false-positive rate is effectively zero so every alert is a real attacker.